/**
\page org_mitk_views_odfmaximaextraction ODF Peak Extraction

This view provides the user interface to extract the principal diffusion direction of tensors and the peaks of spherical harmonic ODFs using gradient descent optimization.

The output peaks can for example be used for streamline tractography.

The spherical harmonics convention of an SH image (MITK/MRtrix or FSL/Dipy) is a property of the image: it is chosen in the reader options when an SH image of another toolkit is loaded, and the peak extraction uses it.

A NIfTI or NRRD file is read as an SH image when "SH Image" is chosen as reader in the open dialog. SH files of earlier MITK versions that store one vector of coefficients per voxel of a 3D image open as plain multi-component images unless "SH Image" is chosen. With the reader option "SH convention" left at "auto", their coefficients are taken over unchanged in MITK's own FSL definition, the basis those versions used. Do not choose "FSL/Dipy" for these files: that option converts files written by dipy (its descoteaux07 basis) and gives wrong peaks for them. A file in this layout that the current MITK saved from an SH image with the generic image writer (only when that writer is chosen in the save dialog) usually holds MITK's legacy basis instead; choose "MITK legacy (MRtrix 0.2)" for it.

For SH images, a peak is dropped if it is below the relative threshold (a fraction of the largest peak of the voxel) or the absolute threshold, or closer than the angular threshold to a larger peak that is kept, so that each fiber direction appears once; at most "Max. Peaks" are kept. Voxels with an isotropic ODF, where no direction stands out, get no peak. For tensor images, the threshold is an FA threshold (voxels with a lower FA get no direction), and only it and the vector normalization act: without normalization the principal eigenvector is scaled by the FA. The other parameters are disabled while a tensor image is selected.

An optional mask restricts the extraction to its non-zero voxels; it must have the geometry of the input image.

The extraction runs in the background: the application stays usable, and the start button is disabled until the peak image appears as a new node below the input image. A running computation cannot be stopped: closing the view lets it run to its end and drops its result, and quitting the application waits until it has ended.


*/
